{"type":"assistant","uuid":"91234375-a747-4d14-b88f-8d86162441ef","session_id":"ad55234f-e1cb-4ef3-9e71-145a7fe2b79d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"91234375-a747-4d14-b88f-8d86162441ef","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the bug. Let me analyze the `processDegreeInput` method:\n\n```java\nprivate void processDegreeInput(EditText degreeText, SeekArc seekArc) {\n    int previousDegree = degree;\n    String degreeStr = degreeText.getText().toString().trim();\n\n    if (degreeStr.isEmpty()) {\n        degree = (degree == 0) ? (int) (seekArc.getProgress() * 3.6) : previousDegree;\n    } else {\n        try {\n            degree = Integer.parseInt(degreeStr);\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            degreeText.setText(getResources().getString(R.string.zero));\n            seekArc.setProgress(0);\n            toastInvalidValueMessage();\n            return;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (degree > 360 || degree < 0) {\n        degreeText.setText(getResources().getString(R.string.zero));\n        seekArc.setProgress(0);\n        toastInvalidValueMessage();\n    } else {\n        seekArc.setProgress((int) (degree / 3.6));\n        editEnter = true;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe issue is with how empty inputs are handled. When a user clears a text field, the code checks if `degree == 0` to decide whether to use the seek bar position or keep the previous value. But this logic is broken because the `degree` variable is shared across all servos, so after setting servo 1 to 90 degrees, the variable holds 90, and when the user clears servo 2's input, it incorrectly uses the seek bar position instead of preserving servo 2's previous value. The problem is that the shared `degree` variable gets overwritten when editing different servos, so when one servo clears its input, it incorrectly uses the seekArc value from whichever servo was last touched instead of its own previous value. The fix requires passing the EditText itself to `processDegreeInput` so each servo can restore its own prior degree, and then in `onOptionsItemSelected`, I need to update the case handlers to pass both the EditText and SeekArc instances to the method.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering whether tracking each servo's previous degree separately is the right approach. Looking back at the bug description, it mentions a cached value being used incorrectly when the user enters a value for one servo and then interacts with another—this suggests the issue might be simpler than I thought, possibly just about which servo's previous value gets restored when clearing inputs.\n\nLet me trace through the actual bug scenario: when the user enters a value for servo 1, then clears servo 2's input and hits enter, servo 2 incorrectly gets set to servo 1's value instead of remaining at its original value. This happens because the code reuses a shared `degree` variable across servo operations, causing the wrong previous value to be restored.\n\nThe real problem is that `degree` is a single shared field tracking the last modified servo's angle, so when clearing a different servo's input, it pulls that shared value instead of the target servo's own previous state. I need separate tracking for each servo's last degree value—`degree1`, `degree2`, `degree3`, `degree4`—so each servo restores its own correct previous value when cleared.\n\nThe fix involves adding individual fields for each servo's previous angle, initializing them to 0 when the activity starts, and updating them whenever a servo's value is set through the seek bar or text input.\n\nNow I need to wire up the listeners to capture the degree value from the EditText before processing, then pass it to the method that calculates and applies the new angle. Writing the helper methods...\n\nNow I'm setting up the seek bar listeners for servos 1 and 2, which update the degree text fields when the user adjusts them. Writing UI event handlers... Writing UI event handlers... Writing input handlers... I'm reconsidering the design here—the bug description emphasizes that each servo should maintain its own value independently, so when a user clears a text field, it should revert to that specific servo's previous state rather than borrowing from another servo's history. My approach of using individual previous degree variables for each servo handles this correctly, keeping the state properly isolated per servo.\n\nFor initialization, the text fields start at \"0\" and seek arcs at 0, so all previous values begin at 0. I'll store the degree values as double since the seek arcs use fractional progress multiplied by 3.6 to convert to degrees, and that's what gets written to the text fields.\n\nNow I need to trace through the actual flow: when a user enters a value and presses enter, the EditText triggers `onEditorAction` which calls `processDegreeInput`. That method updates the `previousDegree` field and then sets the SeekArc progress with `editEnter = true`, which prevents the SeekArc's listener from updating the text field in a loop. If the user clears the text field and presses enter again, `degreeText.getText().toString()` returns an empty string, and the current buggy code tries to recover by checking if the value is zero or using the SeekArc position—but this loses track of the servo's actual previous value.\n\nThe fix needs to preserve the servo's own `previousDegree` separately and use it when the input is cleared, rather than relying on a global `degree` variable that could belong to any servo.\n\nNow I'm planning the implementation: add individual `previousDegree` fields for each servo, initialize them to 0 when text fields start at \"0\", update them in `onEditorAction` before processing input, and refactor `processDegreeInput` to accept the correct previous value for the specific servo being edited. Writing the fix now... Updating the seek arc listeners to use the new method signature... Now I'm setting up the editor action listeners and click handlers for the degree text fields, following the same pattern as before.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
